Certainly! Modifying the regular expression used to extract WHOIS information can allow you to retrieve different pieces of information about a domain name. Let's take a look at an example:

Suppose you wanted to retrieve the status of a domain name (either "Active" or "Inactive"), which is typically included in the WHOIS record as the "Domain Status" field. To modify the regular expression to extract this information, you would need to modify the "if" statement within the "foreach" loop that processes the WHOIS lines. Specifically, you would want to add a new condition to check for the "Domain_Status" field and capture its value in the `%whois_data` hash. Here's an example modification you could make to the script:

```
foreach my $line (@whois_lines) {
    if ($line =~ /^\s*(.+? )\s*:\s*(.+)\s*$/) {
        my ($key, $value) = ($1, $2);
        $key =~ s/\s/_/g;
        $whois_data{$key} = $value;

        # add condition to capture Domain_Status field
        if ($key eq 'Domain_Status') {
            $whois_data{'Domain_Status'} = $value;
        }
    }
}
```

With this modification, the script will now retrieve the value of the "Domain_Status" field if it's included in the WHOIS record. Once you've saved the updated script, you can run it as before to retrieve the modified set of WHOIS information.

