- `\b` is a word boundary anchor that matches the position between a word character (as defined by `\w`) and a non-word character (as defined by `\W`). This ensures that the regular expression will only match the word "hello" and not partial matches such as "hellothere". - `hello` is the literal string that we want to match. - `\b` is another word boundary anchor that ensures that the regular expression will only match the word "hello" and not partial matches such as "helloing". Now, let's go through the code with some comments:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/perl

use strict;
use warnings;

my $input_file = "input.txt";
my $output_file = "output.txt";
my $replacement_word = "hi";
my $replace_count = 0;

# Open input and output files
open(IN, "<", $input_file) or die "Unable to open $input_file: $! ";
open(OUT, ">", $output_file) or die "Unable to open $output_file: $! ";

while(my $line = <IN>) {
    # Replace all occurrences of "hello" with "hi"
    $line =~ s/\bhello\b/$replacement_word/g;

    # Count the number of replacements made in this line
    $replace_count += ($line =~ s/\bhello\b/$replacement_word/g);

    # Write modified line to output file
    print OUT $line;
}

# Close input and output files
close(IN);
close(OUT);

# Print total number of replacements made to console
