import java.util.Arrays;

public class FourthLargestElement {
     public static void main(String[] args) {
         int[] arr = { 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 };
         System.out.println("Input Array: " + Arrays.toString(arr));
         int n = arr.length;
         Arrays.sort(arr);
         int fourthLargest = arr[0];
         int count = 1;
         for (int I = 1; I < n; i++) {
             if (arr[i] != arr[i - 1]) {
                 count++;
             }
             if (count == 4) {
                 fourthLargest = arr[i];
                 break;
             }
         }
         System.out.println("Fourth Largest Element: " + fourthLargest);
     }
}
```

In this program, we first create an array of integers and sort it using the built-in `Arrays.sort()` method.

After that, we declare and initialize two new variables: "fourthLargest" and "count". "fourthLargest" variable is initialized to the first element of the sorted array and "count" variable is initialized to 1.

Next, we run a loop through the sorted array and check if each element is different from the previous one. If the current element is different from the previous element, we increase the value of the "count" variable.

If the count variable becomes 4, we break the loop and assign the current element value to the "fourthLargest" variable.

Finally, we print out the value of the "fourthLargest" variable as the output of the program.

This approach is useful when we have to deal with an unsorted array and still want to find the fourth largest element.

